  "about": "Rules for attack paths (attack_path.py). A path = package capability (what the code does) -> who runs it -> our capabilities and assets. Paths explain; they never change the verdict. package_capabilities: for each capability kind that code analysis (package_caps.py) reports, a description and how it reaches our side (reach: process = anything as the OS user, file_read, file_write, environment_read; matches reached_by of our capabilities). phases: when the code runs -> execution context. findings + capabilities: fallback table for when the code cannot be seen (a removed malicious release, source that was not scanned) and for evidence that is not code (instructions aimed at the agent). Evidence id -> fallback capability id (null = not linked into a path). Every rule that leaves evidence must appear in findings (checked by guard/rule_check.py). context in capabilities: a key of contexts (agent = something the agent does, so agent permission rules apply; by_location = decided by the file the evidence is in). sources: for each origin of a record (package = the package in the command, dependency = its dependency, direct = git or URL, hook / mcp_server / plugin = agent extensions), the execution context to use instead of by_location (context, null = by evidence location) and how to cut the path at the choke point (cut).",
